A clique is a set of vertices in which every pair of vertices is connected by an edge. In other words, it's a group of nodes where any two nodes can be connected by an edge.
For example, consider a graph with four nodes labeled A, B, C, and D. The following sets of three nodes form a clique:
- {A, B, C}
- {B, C, D}
- {C, D, A}

The above sets of three nodes are called cliques because they contain all possible combinations of three nodes. For instance, if we take the node A, then we can connect it to B, C, or D. Similarly, if we take the node B, then we can connect it to A, C, or D. If we take the node C, then we can connect it to A, B, or D. Finally, if we take the node D, then we can connect it to A, B, or C.
